the only  g7 country whose greenhouse gas  emissions have increased  since the paris agreement the oil and  gas industry is a big emitter but also a  huge  economic driver in canada and as eric  sorensen reports that makes shifting to  a green economy  a big challenge in the capital of  canada's biggest oil producing province  one more home is going solar a symbol of  an alternative energy future  alex mialovich used to work in oil and  gas but saw an opportunity seven months  ago to pivot  to installing solar panels it's been  pretty uh  non-stop busy lately we've got two or  three jobs going a week with my crew  so it's been actually really good the  company he works for kuby renewable  energy  is already changing the face of edmonton  it installed solar cells on almost  700 windows to power a portion of the  city's convention center  it's super satisfying turning on a  system and seeing all that power being  generated just by the sun  but the transition will be disruptive  for an industry as massive as alberta's  oil and gas  sector in fort mcmurray there is no  imminent green economy to rescue a  community that depends enormously on the  nearby oil sands  and has seen jobs lost to a pandemic on  top of the oil slump  it's just taken an immense toll on the  psyche of the city  in terms of the optimism that we used to  feel in canada a shift away from oil and  gas will not be felt evenly  alberta saskatchewan and newfoundland  and labrador account for most oil sector  jobs  specifically oil and gas extraction  represents 1.5 percent of the national  labor force  but in communities like fort mcmurray  and cold lake it's 25 to 30 percent  having gainful employment is immensely  uh huge for them they they need this  work ottawa sees the job crunch coming  proposing 250 million dollars over three  years  to upskill and redeploy workers in  growing industries  and 55 million to support in part a just  transition for workers in sectors like  energy we're now starting to see more  of a proactive role by the federal  government to make direct investments  in the clean energy transition that we  ought to focus those in the communities  that are going to bear that brunt of the  impact the uncertainty in the oil patch  is real  but so are the small examples where  workers are being retrained  a lot of the skills that i've developed  over the years in oil and gas  you know i can bring them over to  renewables and still use that kind of  stuff  alex mielovich says there's room for  more workers to retrain as he has  for a job that looks like it'll be  around for a while  eric sorensen global news toronto  another funeral for a black man killed  by the police  still ahead remembering dante wright  family and friends gathered in  minneapolis today to honor the life of  dante wright  the 20 year old unarmed black man was  shot and killed by a white police  officer during a traffic stop wright's  mother spoke to her son just moments  before that deadly encounter  today she and his father were overcome  with emotion  when he walked in the room he lit up the  room he was a  brother a jokester  he was loved by so many he's gonna be so  missed  take your time katie words can't even  explain how  i feel right now you know  that was my son  wright was remembered as an adoring  father to his one-year-old son who bears  his name  hundreds came to the minneapolis church  for the funeral including  families of other black men killed in  police custody and at one point members  of george  floyd's family were called to the front  showing solidarity  with the grieving family cashing in on  hand-me-downs next the sustainable  shopping boom  nasa's perseverance rover just made  oxygen on mars  it's a giant leap that may one day turn  science fiction into fact  allowing humans to survive on the red  planet perseverance used an instrument  called moxie to convert carbon dioxide  into breathable air  after an hour moxie produced about 5.4  grams of oxygen that's enough to keep an  astronaut alive and healthy  for about 10 minutes of normal activity  since the pandemic began much of the  fashion industry has taken a hit  but not the resale sector the online  market for used clothes is growing  faster than the market for new ones  as robin gill reports a younger  generation is reassessing both its  social values  and the bottom line laurel grad has a  side  hustle i'm a professional shopper in a  way a nurse by day  and a thrift seeker at night i think  it's slowly turning into a career  i didn't know i would have the 31 year  old spends her free time perusing thrift  stores  looking for deals on trendy clothes and  then reselling them  online she's made some serious cash on  sites like facebook marketplace  and apps like poshmark in our first year  of operations here in canada we  have had 1.4 million active users so  that's definitely shows  the the hunger and the appetite for the  resale market poshmark launched in 2019  but there are  similar apps that are pushing the used  economy  within the next 10 years what we're  going to see is  the global market for these second-hand  purchases is going to go up to  they're seeing 80 billion dollars  worldwide consumer trends show a younger  generation wants to be more socially  conscious  while saving consumers want to be more  ecologically conscious  obviously if you buy second hand you're  using resources that exist rather than  purchasing new stuff  you're choosing to wanting to extend the  item  the life of the item thrift stores have  been around for generations  they rely on canadians to clean out  their closets and donate the items  many thrift stores help fund outreach  programs for low-income families  so are the thrift seekers robbing them  of their bottom line  that was the dilemma facing laurel grad  until she did some research  they need us to buy it so 99  of their income comes from buyers and so  they want people to come in buy the  clothes and then that can go back into  the community her conscience clear  she's now thinking about giving up  nursing her business on the side  is growing my hope is that i make five  to ten thousand in a  month from selling second-hand clothing  what started out as a hobby to make a  little extra cash  just may be a full-time job after all  robin gill global news  vancouver and that is global national  for this thursday i'm donna friesen  tonight's here in canada is the hamlet  of delau  southwest of brandon manitoba thanks for  watching robin gill will be at the  anchor desk tomorrow and i will see you  again on saturday for the new reality  take care of yourselves bye-bye  [Music]
 

